WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:05.920
Amazon is named after the largest river in&nbsp;
the world - you can see it in the business'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.880
early logo. The river may flow through&nbsp;
the tropical forests of South America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:18.160
but it seems the company has an even bigger reach.&nbsp;
Amazon went from selling books in 1995 to selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.360
everything. Jeff Bezos believes its success&nbsp;
can be boiled down to a philosophy he described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:29.360
in an interview with CNBC during the early days.&nbsp;
If there's one thingAamazon.com is about it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:35.920
obsessive attention to the customer experience&nbsp;
end to end. A big part of focusing on the customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.120
experience is in the area of delivery. Packages&nbsp;
often arrive well before they're supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.080
even if you don't have Prime. And if you do,&nbsp;
deliveries are getting faster: going from two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:57.840
days to one, to same-day shipping. But that&nbsp;
all pales in comparison to what comes next.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:04.880
This giant floating airship is deploying packages&nbsp;
carried by Amazon drones except it isn't real.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.600
Here's the original rendering. The Japanese&nbsp;
filmmaker who made this told me he posted it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.600
a joke. It just so happens that a few years ago,&nbsp;
Amazon did file a patent for a floating warehouse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.880
Imagine a fulfillment center stocked with products&nbsp;
in the air flying to places where demand is high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.320
like sporting events. When the airship needs to&nbsp;
restock, a smaller one will meet it in the skies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.600
bringing along staff, drones, fuel, and&nbsp;
inventory. Companies file patents all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.840
time but it doesn't necessarily translate&nbsp;
into anything real. What this does show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.800
is Amazon's ambitions for its drones and it&nbsp;
started out with deliveries here in the UK.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.680
Two hours north of London, in the Cambridge&nbsp;
area, Richard Barnes became the first customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.800
to receive an order by drone as part of Amazon's&nbsp;
test trial. After putting in his purchase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.520
a fulfillment center received it, then packaged&nbsp;
up the items. The maximum weight the drone can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.880
carry is five pounds which doesn't sound like&nbsp;
a lot, although Amazon points out 75 to 90&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.440
percent of its orders are under that limit.&nbsp;
The drone makes its way down an automated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.920
track before rising like a helicopter to nearly&nbsp;
400 feet. It's guided by GPS to the destination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.440
and uses sense and avoid technology to detect the&nbsp;
surroundings. Amazon promises Prime Air will make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:26.160
deliveries in 30 minutes or less. Since that&nbsp;
trial in 2016, the drone's design has changed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:32.080
The latest version has a noticeable tilt allowing&nbsp;
use of all six propellers to move forward. Tests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.960
are still being carried out in the UK and around&nbsp;
the world and they could be coming to America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:43.520
Earlier this year, Amazon received U.S.&nbsp;
federal approval to operate as a drone company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.000
joining two others: Wing Aviation, owned&nbsp;
by Google's parent company Alphabet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.080
and UPS Flight Forward. Getting the okay from&nbsp;
the FAA wasn't easy. Amazon had to prove its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:59.920
operations were safe. The drones are jam-packed&nbsp;
with three types of sensors: visual, thermal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:05.520
and sonar. No sensor by itself can do it all&nbsp;
and let me give you an example: fluffy dogs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:12.160
are invisible to sonar. Besides looking out for&nbsp;
cute fluffy pets, the sensors can detect when a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:17.840
person gets too close and very significantly can&nbsp;
spot things as thin as clotheslines in a backyard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.680
or wires. Still, when we're talking about an&nbsp;
unmanned aerial vehicle, it's not without risks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.960
In case of a catastrophic failure, another patent&nbsp;
shows the drone breaking apart deliberately in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:34.080
midair, splitting on the ground and in a lake,&nbsp;
before a controlled collision into a tree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.040
The reasoning is if it's going to crash, better&nbsp;
it be in small chunks rather than a big one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:44.000
Another option is to rely on parachutes for&nbsp;
the landing. The California startup Zipline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.400
uses that method to deliver life-saving&nbsp;
blood supplies to rural areas in Rwanda.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.680
While continuing to develop the technology&nbsp;
to make it as safe as possible, Amazon has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.640
to figure out the logistics of making drone&nbsp;
deliveries within the tight 30-minute window.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.200
Fulfillment centers are usually located on the&nbsp;
outskirts of cities to accommodate their large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.240
size yet this is inconvenient for deliveries into&nbsp;
cities where a growing number of people live.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.880
One solution is constructing a different kind&nbsp;
of fulfillment center that can be tucked into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:18.320
downtown, blending into the surroundings - such&nbsp;
as this cylindrical building or a beehive-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:23.840
structure that resembles London's iconic Gherkin&nbsp;
skyscraper. This can also act as a command center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.360
similar to a flight controller at an airport&nbsp;
managing traffic as drones come and go. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.640
flying machines can travel up to 15 miles before&nbsp;
running out of power. Instead of heading back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:39.440
the fulfillment center to replenish the batteries,&nbsp;
they can do so on a structure like a lamppost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.480
which would be reconfigured to allow for charging&nbsp;
and also double as a pickup and drop-off point for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:49.200
packages. The drones would communicate with a&nbsp;
central control system either through a wired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:54.960
or wireless connection, bringing free wi-fi to the&nbsp;
area. Amazon thinks this kind of setup can extend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:01.200
to pretty much any tall structure such as parking&nbsp;
decks, church steeples, and cell towers. So there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:06.400
are many options to consider and a combination&nbsp;
of any or all of them could very well be used.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:11.920
Although many regulatory hurdles still have to be&nbsp;
cleared, if and when drones take off on a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:18.160
scale, they will change our idea of delivery. We&nbsp;
will no longer think in terms of days or hours but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:23.440
minutes. I mentioned the company's guiding&nbsp;
philosophy is the focus on customer experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.400
but there is another philosophy at Amazon's&nbsp;
headquarters in Seattle. Bezos named one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:33.680
the buildings Day One, treating every day like&nbsp;
the first day of a startup, in order to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:39.440
quickly and focus on results. If it's day one&nbsp;
at Amazon, then they're just getting started.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:43.840
Thanks so much for watching, I'm Cindy&nbsp;
Pom. So what do you think of Amazon's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.640
drone delivery service? Do you think the best&nbsp;
option is airships a beehive like structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:51.920
downtown? Let me know your thoughts. If&nbsp;
you like what you saw, give it a like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:56.240
and don't forget to subscribe to my channel. Thank&nbsp;
you as always to my Patrons for your continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

